{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>If it be found, it shall be found.</b> The reason for the doubling is: one instance speaks for itself — that witnesses came [testifying] that he stole it — and the second is to say that they found it intact in his possession. But absent this, he would be liable to pay fourfold or fivefold, for whenever [the stolen object] is not in his hand, the presumption is that it is sold or slaughtered. And the verse did not say [he pays only] double except when it is found. And you should know that it is not specifically [if he] sold or slaughtered, but the same ruling applies [if he] gave it away or lost it — anything that departed from his domain — and so it is explicitly proven in the Talmud (Bava Kama 79b), and likewise the same applies if he consecrated it [to the Temple]. And I saw [reason] to infer from here that if one stole from an idolater, the law of ״וְאִם אֵין לוֹ וְנִמְכַּר בִּגְנֵבָתוֹ״ that is stated just below does not apply — from the fact that adjacent to ״וְנִמְכַּר בִּגְנֵבָתוֹ״ the verse states ״אִם הִמָּצֵא תִמָּצֵא וְגוֹ׳ שְׁנַיִם יְשַׁלֵּם״, and the verse says regarding the law of double restitution — ״לְרֵעֵהוּ״ and not to an idolater, as they of blessed memory expounded (Mekhilta). From this you have learned that the law of ״וְנִמְכַּר בִּגְנֵבָתוֹ״ stated in this matter — the verse speaks only of one who steals from a Jew, and not from an idolater, and not from consecrated property. And these are the words of the Rambam in chapter 3 of the Laws of Theft, and they found no source for him there — and behold, here it is."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:32:23.012151608Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}